2. FILES CHANGED TODAY   (all backups in sys/)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
config.g       G31 P500 X0 Y0 Z-0.025   (was Z0.7)     bak: config.g.bak-trigheight
bed.g          P1/P2 probe points swapped to match M671 bak: bed.g.bak-preswap
project.txt    Z motor order corrected                  bak: project.txt.bak-zorder
mesh.g         NEW - G32-then-G29 mesh macro            (no prior version)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Z PROBE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TRIGGER HEIGHT = -0.025mm  (uncertainty +/-0.025)
Method: G31 Z0, G30 at centre, then jog down onto 0.10mm paper (micrometer
measured). Paper free at Z0.15, locked at Z0.10 -> contact ~Z0.025.
NEGATIVE is correct and expected: the ALPS is a strain gauge, so the nozzle
presses ~0.025mm into the bed before the trigger threshold is crossed.

*** REDO AT TEMPERATURE once the PT1000 is replaced. Nozzle thermal expansion
    shifts this 0.05-0.10mm. The current value is COLD and is fine for geometry
    work but must not be carried into printing.
*** To narrow the +/-0.025: repeat the paper test in 0.01 steps between Z0.15
    and Z0.10. Do it at the same time as the hot recalibration, not twice.

P5 vs P9  ->  RESOLVED: P9, and it is load-bearing.
RRF only auto-runs deployprobe.g / retractprobe.g for deployable probe types
(type 9). Those macros are what assert the ALPS enable pin (M42 P1 S1/S0).
Switch to P5 and the enable never asserts and the probe goes dead.
DO NOT "tidy" this to P5.

Probe output is BINARY (0 / 1000), confirmed by a 21-step scan at 0.01mm
intervals - it sits at 0 then snaps to 1000. So the P500 threshold is not
discriminating anything; any value 1..999 behaves identically.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. bed.g MOTOR ORDER BUG  (found and fixed)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RRF pairs G30 P<n> to the n-th M671 coordinate. bed.g had P1 and P2 swapped
relative to M671, so G32 was applying the front-left correction to the rear
motor and vice versa.

Invisible on a near-level bed - all three corrections come out near-equal and
swapping two equal numbers changes nothing. It would only bite once the bed was
genuinely out of tram, as failure to converge.

Corner-to-driver mapping CONFIRMED PHYSICALLY by M584 single-driver jog:
  driver 0.0 = front-right   M671 entry 0 (424.75, -8.75)
  driver 0.1 = rear          M671 entry 1 (201, 415)
  driver 0.2 = front-left    M671 entry 2 (-22.75, -8.75)
config.g's comment was correct. project.txt was wrong and has been fixed.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. FRAME GEOMETRY SURVEY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
METHOD
Bed is granite = rigid flat reference. Self-weight sag of a 20mm granite strip
over a 400mm span computes to 0.0053mm - fifty times too small to matter, so the
slab cannot be the source of anything measured here. A rigid plate on a 3-point
mount defines a plane exactly, so after G32 the entire heightmap residual is
MACHINE geometry, not bed.

HEIGHTMAP (G29, 7x7, 49 points, X10:390 Y16:380)
min -0.026  max 0.521  mean 0.118  deviation 0.116
Row 0 = Y16 (FRONT), row 6 = Y380 (REAR). RRF writes rows from Ymin upward.

   0.250  0.223  0.211  0.181  0.176  0.167  0.228     <- Y16   front
   0.107  0.091  0.081  0.068  0.067  0.069  0.148
   0.021  0.016  0.009  0.010  0.011  0.032  0.124
  -0.017 -0.026 -0.021  0.000  0.018  0.051  0.158
  -0.011 -0.014  0.011  0.048  0.075  0.119  0.240
   0.021  0.039  0.074  0.120  0.169  0.218  0.361
   0.102  0.138  0.189  0.252  0.304  0.369  0.521     <- Y380  rear
  X10                                            X390

Positive = bed reads HIGH = toolhead sits LOW there (bed is flat, so it is the
gantry that moves). Sign confirmed independently by dropping the bed 0.400mm and
watching the DTI fall.

SURFACE DECOMPOSITION (least squares, % of spread explained)
  plane  (1,x,y)             13.9%   <- G32 already removed tilt
  twist  (1,x,y,xy)          25.2%
