  agreement rms 0.019mm, max 0.039mm
  both fit a parabola: vertices Y178 (DTI) vs Y181 (mesh), curvature within 16%
  return-to-zero checked twice: 0.000 then +0.010 -> DTI floor is +/-0.01
  no hysteresis: Y35 read -0.260 approached from both directions

CORNERS (relative to X200 Y195 = 0)
                        mesh     DTI
  front-left  (15,15)  +0.248   NOT MEASURED (session diverted)
  front-right (385,15) +0.223   +0.180
  rear-left   (15,375) +0.105   +0.125
  rear-right  (385,375)+0.509   +0.530

  front edge spread 0.025mm   <- level
  rear  edge spread 0.404mm   <- the fault
  DTI rear-edge spread 0.405mm - agrees with mesh to 0.001mm

RAIL HEIGHTS (sign flipped, +ve = rail higher; from heightmap columns X10/X390)
     Y     left    right     R-L
    16   -0.250   -0.228   +0.022
    77   -0.107   -0.148   -0.041
   137   -0.021   -0.124   -0.103
   198   +0.017   -0.158   -0.175
   259   +0.011   -0.240   -0.251
   319   -0.021   -0.361   -0.340
   380   -0.102   -0.521   -0.419

The right rail drop ACCELERATES rearward: -0.197 over the first 182mm, -0.244
over the second. A uniform twist would be linear. This is a droop or a mislocated
support at the rear, not the whole assembly being cocked.

Rail crown vs own chord: left +0.193, right +0.217 (common mode - ignore)
X beam bow after removing rail tilt: +0.082mm / 380mm = 0.21 mm/m (good)

NOT A LOOSE JOINT
Rear joint is an angle bracket with a large gusset and 4x M6, plus tower
fasteners outboard. 0.42mm is far too large for elastic deflection of that.
Measurements agree: zero hysteresis on reversal, and the reading returned to
-0.225 at Y15 after a full traverse. A joint working loose would not repeat to
5 microns. Conclusion: built-in assembly geometry.
0.42mm over 400mm is 1 part in 1000 - below what a square and rule will hold on
a frame this size.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. SHIM SCHEDULE   -  RIGHT Y RAIL ONLY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Basis: MGN12, 500mm rail, 25mm hole pitch, 12.5mm end distance, 20 holes.
Fastened at the two centre holes then every other hole outward = 10 fixings.
Rail centre assumed aligned to Y travel centre (machine_Y = rail_pos - 55).
Scale factor 550/380 = 1.447 (rail centres 550mm vs 380mm probe span).
Zeroed on fastener 1 - the front is the datum, it is already level.

 #   rail mm   machine Y   exact    USE     build from
 1      37.5      -17.5    0.000    0.00    (none - datum)
 2      87.5       32.5    0.068    0.05    0.05
 3     137.5       82.5    0.142    0.15    0.1 + 0.05
 4     187.5      132.5    0.220    0.20    0.2
 5     237.5      182.5    0.304    0.30    0.2 + 0.1
 6     262.5      207.5    0.348    0.35    0.2 + 0.1 + 0.05
 7     312.5      257.5    0.440    0.45    0.4 + 0.05
 8     362.5      307.5    0.537    0.55    0.4 + 0.1 + 0.05
 9     412.5      357.5    0.639    0.65    0.4 + 0.2 + 0.05
10     462.5      407.5    0.746    0.75    0.4 + 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.05

total front-to-rear 0.746mm

Underlying fit (unscaled, before x1.447):
  shim(Y) = -0.01479 + 0.000932*Y + 7.215e-07*Y^2     residual 0.0026mm
  Use this if fastener positions turn out different - it is slightly progressive,
  a straight taper leaves a few hundredths behind.

RULES
- Shims must span the FULL rail width (12mm). Washer-sized shims let the rail
  bridge and deform locally - defeats the point and wears the bearings.
- Grade it, never step it. Every fixing gets its own thickness.
- Front fastener is the datum. All correction is added going rearward.
- Do NOT shim the left rail. Do NOT touch the X beam.

CAVEATS
- Fasteners 1 and 10 (Y-17.5 and Y+407.5) are EXTRAPOLATED - the mesh only
  covered Y16-380. They are also the two that set the overall slope, so they are
  the worst two to be guessing at. #10 is the largest shim in the stack.
- Rail placement is assumed, not measured. Sensitivity is low (+/-20mm of
  placement = 0.025mm of shim, below stock granularity) so this is second order.
- Rail spacing at 550mm is the sensitive input: 420mm vs 500mm swings the rear
  shim by 0.109mm. Re-measure if unsure.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Z SCALE DISCREPANCY  ***RESOLVED 21/08/2026 - Z IS CORRECT***
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Commanding a 0.400mm Z move produced 0.4425mm of DTI deflection (+10.6%). Two
candidates were open: wrong Z steps/mm, or the DTI over-reading.

RESOLVED BY DIRECT MEASUREMENT after a power cycle:
  commanded Z50 -> Z20   = 30.000mm
  caliper, fixed frame reference to bed surface:
      at Z50   81.97 mm
      at Z20   51.96 mm
      measured travel 30.01 mm   -> error 0.03%, i.e. caliper resolution

*** M92 Z800 IS CORRECT. The bed arm linkage theory is dead. ***
*** The heightmap is in REAL MILLIMETRES. The shim table in section 6 stands
    exactly as written - NO 10% rescale. ***

The 10.6% therefore belongs to the Mitutoyo, almost certainly a stylus that is
not the length the indicator is calibrated for (lever DTIs scale directly with
stylus length; a short stylus over-reads). Dividing every DTI reading by 1.1062
improves agreement with the mesh from rms 0.0231 to 0.0187mm - a 19% improvement,
supportive but not decisive on its own. The caliper check is the real evidence.

PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE: that indicator reads ~11% high until the stylus is
identified. Fine for finding shape and nulling to zero; do not trust it for
absolute magnitudes. Check the stylus length against the indicator's rated length
before using it as a measuring instrument again.

One point remains a genuine outlier: front-right (X385 Y15) sits -0.043 from the
mesh as read, and -0.060 corrected - it gets WORSE with the correction, unlike
everything else. Front-left was never captured. Worth re-measuring both corners
before relying on the front edge being level to 0.022mm.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. ORDER LIST
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEW, arising from this session:

 SHIM STOCK - brass or stainless, for the right Y rail
   Minimum needed:   0.05mm x7,  0.1mm x5,  0.2mm x5,  0.4mm x4
   Order at least 2x that - they get cut, dropped and re-made.
   Sheet stock is more useful than pre-cut washers: needs to span the full 12mm
   rail width with an M3 clearance hole or slot. A shim assortment pack in
   0.05/0.1/0.2/0.4 covers it.
   NOTE: hold until the Z scale question in section 7 is settled - if Z is the
   culprit every thickness rises ~10%.

 FEELER GAUGE SET - metric, including 0.05 and 0.10 blades
   Trigger height was measured on paper, which is why it carries +/-0.025.
   Two known blade thicknesses give two independent estimates that should agree,
   which also validates the drag judgement. Needed for the hot recalibration.

ALREADY OUTSTANDING (see project.txt PROCUREMENT - not repeated in full):
   Granite slab replacement, hotend PT1000, F1 3.15A 5V MDR fuse,
   Key-Bak reel, cable glands, 20mm momentary push button.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. NEXT STEPS, IN ORDER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. Resolve the Z scale question (section 7). Blocks the shim thicknesses.
 2. Re-run G32 + G29 AFTER A POWER CYCLE and confirm the corners reproduce.
    Everything here rests on ONE heightmap taken in a single session with the
    machine never re-homed from cold. Do not cut ten shims on one capture.
 3. Anchor the rear extrapolation: DTI readings at Y380-390 are inside the
    travel envelope and would replace fastener 10's predicted value with a
    measured one. Y-17.5 cannot be reached, but it is the zero reference so an
    error there only shifts everything uniformly.
 4. Measure front-left corner with the DTI - the one corner never captured.
 5. Shim the right rail per section 6. Requires removing rear insulation.
 6. Re-check the FRONT corners afterwards - freeing a structural joint on a frame
    this size can redistribute more than intended.
 7. Re-run G32 + G29 to verify the correction landed.
 8. After PT1000 arrives: hot trigger height recalibration, then re-mesh.

Note on timing: the machine cannot print until the PT1000 arrives, so nothing is
blocked by leaving the frame as-is. The replacement slab will force a re-tram and
re-mesh anyway - doing frame work BEFORE that lands inside a measurement cycle
you are already paying for.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. THINGS THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT ME OUT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- RRF heightmap rows run Ymin FIRST. Row 0 is the FRONT, not the rear.
- The ALPS enable is de-asserted between probes by retractprobe.g. Reading
  sensors.probes[0].value outside a probe cycle returns 0 regardless of load -
  assert M42 P1 S1 first or the reading is meaningless.
- Heightmap "positive" means bed high, which with a flat bed means the GANTRY IS
  LOW there. The correction is to RAISE that corner, not lower it.
- move.compensation type must read "none" before any geometry measurement, or
  the machine is silently correcting the thing you are trying to measure.
  M561 at the top of bed.g clears it.
- move.calibration.initial.deviation reads 0 both when G32 has never run and
  when it converged perfectly. It cannot distinguish them - run G32, do not
  trust an inherited value.
- bed.g exits its loop on convergence OR the 15-iteration cap. Completing G32 is
  NOT proof it converged. mesh.g re-checks; bare G32 does not.

